1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,160
For centuries, the world
called this country Persia.

2
00:00:03,620 --> 00:00:09,188
Then, in nineteen thirty-five, its king
asked everyone to use a new name: Iran.

3
00:00:09,669 --> 00:00:11,766
He picked one date for the change.

4
00:00:11,786 --> 00:00:14,525
March twenty-first. The
first day of spring.

5
00:00:14,955 --> 00:00:16,980
I will tell you why at the end.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,695
First, let me show you a
country you will want to visit.

7
00:00:20,894 --> 00:00:24,402
This is Iran. About ninety-two
million people live here.

8
00:00:24,422 --> 00:00:25,808
The capital is Tehran.

9
00:00:26,286 --> 00:00:30,037
The language is Persian, and you
say hello with one word: salaam.

10
00:00:30,057 --> 00:00:32,436
Dozens of other languages are spoken too.

11
00:00:32,882 --> 00:00:37,325
Prices are spoken in tomans, but
the real currency is the rial.

12
00:00:37,345 --> 00:00:39,258
One toman equals ten rials.

13
00:00:39,616 --> 00:00:42,133
Now, four facts that will surprise you.

14
00:00:42,153 --> 00:00:46,771
Fact one. Nearly ninety percent of
the world's saffron comes from Iran.

15
00:00:47,288 --> 00:00:52,067
Fact two. In winter, you can ride a
gondola from Tehran up to a ski resort,

16
00:00:52,087 --> 00:00:54,839
more than thirty-seven
hundred meters high.

17
00:00:55,333 --> 00:01:00,459
Fact three. Not far away, Mount
Damavand is the highest volcano in Asia.

18
00:01:01,008 --> 00:01:02,899
Fact four. In the Lut Desert,

19
00:01:02,919 --> 00:01:06,984
satellites have measured sand
at over seventy degrees Celsius.

20
00:01:07,004 --> 00:01:10,694
That is one of the hottest
places ever recorded on land.

21
00:01:11,090 --> 00:01:16,429
And yet, people here made ice in the
desert, over two thousand years ago.

22
00:01:16,449 --> 00:01:19,733
They built giant clay
cones called yakhchals,

23
00:01:19,753 --> 00:01:22,029
and kept ice frozen all summer.

24
00:01:22,825 --> 00:01:25,323
Now, where would you go? Start in Isfahan.

25
00:01:25,343 --> 00:01:28,101
An old Persian saying
calls it half the world.

26
00:01:28,343 --> 00:01:32,835
Its great square, Naqsh-e Jahan, is
more than five hundred meters long,

27
00:01:32,855 --> 00:01:35,779
and it is one of the
largest squares on Earth.

28
00:01:36,264 --> 00:01:39,711
Next, go south, to Shiraz.
In the Nasir al-Mulk Mosque,

29
00:01:39,731 --> 00:01:42,863
called the Pink Mosque,
come early in the morning.

30
00:01:42,883 --> 00:01:47,232
The sun shines through stained glass
and paints colors on the carpet.

31
00:01:47,756 --> 00:01:50,174
A short drive away stands Persepolis,

32
00:01:50,194 --> 00:01:53,402
founded by Darius the
Great in five eighteen B C.

33
00:01:53,422 --> 00:01:54,720
By Guinness's count,

34
00:01:54,740 --> 00:02:00,208
around four eighty B C this empire ruled
forty-four percent of all people on Earth.

35
00:02:00,757 --> 00:02:03,797
Then Yazd, a city of
mud brick in the desert.

36
00:02:03,817 --> 00:02:07,150
Tall towers catch the
breeze and cool the houses.

37
00:02:07,841 --> 00:02:13,183
Now, let us eat. The national dish is
chelow kabab: grilled meat, with rice.

38
00:02:13,794 --> 00:02:16,131
But ask any Iranian about tahdig.

39
00:02:16,151 --> 00:02:18,774
The word means the bottom of the pot.

40
00:02:18,794 --> 00:02:23,723
It is the golden, crunchy crust of
rice, and everybody wants a piece.

41
00:02:24,742 --> 00:02:28,980
For dessert, try faloodeh, a
frozen dessert of thin noodles.

42
00:02:28,1000 --> 00:02:33,328
Yes, noodles. It was made with
ice from those old ice houses.

43
00:02:34,275 --> 00:02:39,148
Then there are the carpets. Persian
weavers dye the wool with plants,

44
00:02:39,168 --> 00:02:43,544
like madder and indigo, even
walnut skin and pomegranate skin.

45
00:02:43,564 --> 00:02:47,181
UNESCO listed Persian carpet
weaving in twenty ten.

46
00:02:48,145 --> 00:02:53,178
Poetry matters here too. In Shiraz,
people visit the tomb of the poet Hafez.

47
00:02:53,198 --> 00:02:56,834
Many Iranians ask his book
a question about the future.

48
00:02:56,854 --> 00:03:00,444
They open it at random, and
the page gives the answer.

49
00:03:01,692 --> 00:03:04,086
But the best time to visit is spring.

50
00:03:04,106 --> 00:03:08,913
Nowruz means new day. It begins at the
exact moment of the spring equinox,

51
00:03:08,933 --> 00:03:11,869
and it is more than
three thousand years old.

52
00:03:12,543 --> 00:03:14,734
First, families clean the whole house.

53
00:03:14,754 --> 00:03:18,866
On the last Tuesday night of the
year, people jump over small bonfires,

54
00:03:18,886 --> 00:03:21,214
to leave the old year's bad luck behind.

55
00:03:21,910 --> 00:03:24,363
Then comes the table. The haft-seen:

56
00:03:24,383 --> 00:03:28,554
seven things whose names all
start with the Persian letter S.

57
00:03:28,574 --> 00:03:31,734
Sprouts, an apple, garlic,
vinegar, and sumac.

58
00:03:32,050 --> 00:03:34,196
The new year lasts thirteen days.

59
00:03:34,216 --> 00:03:38,811
On the thirteenth, almost the whole
country goes outside for a picnic.

60
00:03:39,827 --> 00:03:43,204
Now you can see why the king
chose March twenty-first.

61
00:03:43,224 --> 00:03:46,873
Nowruz, the first day of spring,
is Iran's oldest holiday.

62
00:03:47,886 --> 00:03:51,844
And the name Iran was not new.
Iranians had used it for centuries,

63
00:03:51,864 --> 00:03:55,038
while the world said Persia.
In nineteen thirty-five,

64
00:03:55,058 --> 00:03:57,830
the king simply asked
the world to use it too.

65
00:03:58,718 --> 00:04:00,838
Did the world obey? Not entirely.

66
00:04:00,858 --> 00:04:04,601
In nineteen fifty-nine, his
son said both names were fine.

67
00:04:04,621 --> 00:04:08,188
We still say Persian carpets,
and the Persian language.

68
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:12,429
That was Iran. Which country
should we visit next?

69
00:04:12,449 --> 00:04:13,927
Tell me in the comments.

